    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with a set of walnut cabinet carcasses I'm working on. I'm cutting through dovetails, but I'm getting consistent splintering at the tips of the pins every time I finish the cut. The wood is quite stable, so I don't think it's a moisture issue. Any ideas on what might be causing this or how to fix my setup?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The consistent splintering at dovetail tips is most likely caused by catching the wood grain at the corner while exiting the kerf with your saw blade. To fix this, consider lowering your saw height slightly or adjusting your blade angle to ensure you are cutting up the grain rather than across it as you clear the side blade. Additionally, applying a small amount of glue ahead of the knife on the side blade can help seal the wood fibers and reduce tear-out during the final cut. Ensure the cutter's angle is perfectly aligned with the saw's handle for maximum stability before making your next pass.",
      "source": "target",
