    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that scoring trick helps a bit, but it's still happening on some of the finer details. it almost feels like the wood is resisting or being unpredictable in a way that doesn't make sense for the grain. is there any other way to manage the grain density at those points, or maybe some way to reinforce the wood before the final chisel work?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You might be dealing with \"closed grain\" in the softer walnut, which can resist splitting when the fibers are tightly packed and cannot absorb the chisel pressure effectively. To manage this density, you can apply a thin belt of hardened glue over the waste side and clamp it with a scrap piece before removing the knife, which binds the fibers together and gives the tool more purchase. If physical reinforcement still fails on specific outliers, consider finishing the carpentry with a scraper blade that glides rather than cuts, or finish constructing the carcass to avoid working on fresh cut grain patterns in the final steps entirely.",
